===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|ἐννεάς}}, {{m|grc|ἐννεάδος||body of nine}}, from {{m|grc|ἐννέᾰ||nine}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|part of speech=n|id=62478|date=1891|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref> (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁néwn̥||nine}}) + {{m|grc|-ᾰ́ς|pos=''suffix forming abstract nouns of number from numerals''}} or {{m|grc|-ος}}; analysable as {{confix|en|ennea|ad}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈɛnɪad/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ne|ad|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[number#Noun|number]] [[nine]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Proclus|authorlink=Proclus|author2=[[w:Thomas Taylor (neoplatonist)|Thomas Taylor]], transl.|chapter=XXXI|title=The Six Books of Proclus the Platonic Successor, on the Theology of Plato, Translated from the Greek; {{...|to which a Seventh Book is Added, in Order to Supply the Deficiency of Another Book on This Subject, which was Written by Proclus, but since Lost. Also, a Translation from the Greek of Proclus’ Elements of Theology. To which are Added a Translation of the Treatise of Proclus, on Providence and Fate; a Translation of Extracts from His Treatise, Entitled, Ten Doubts Concerning Providence; and a Translation of Extracts from His Treatise on the Subsistence of Evil; as Preserved in the Bibliotheca Gr. of Fabricus. [...]}} Two Volumes|location=London|publisher=Printed for the author, by [[w:Abraham John Valpy|A[braham] J[ohn] Valpy]],{{nb...|Tooke's Court, Chancery Lane. And sold by Messrs. Law and Co.; Longman and Co.; Baldwin and Co.; and all other booksellers.}}|year=1816|volume=II|section=footnote 1|page=211|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=s6AtOsizyhgC&pg=PA211|oclc=28259674|passage=The '''ennead''', according to the [[Pythagorean]]s, circulates all numbers within itself, and there can be no number beyond it. For the natural progression of numbers is as far as to 9, but after it their retrogression takes place. For 10 becomes as it were again the monad. {{...}} Hence it is not possible there should be any elementary number beyond the '''ennead'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Hippolytus|authorlink=Hippolytus of Rome|author2=[[w:John Henry MacMahon|J[ohn] H[enry] MacMahon]]|chapter=XLIII|editors={{w|Alexander Roberts}} and [[w:James Donaldson (classical scholar)|James Donaldson]]|title=[[w:Refutation of All Heresies|The Refutation of All Heresies,{{nb...|[...] With Fragments from His Commentaries on Various Books of Scripture, Translated by the Rev. S[tewart] D[ingwall] F[ordyce] Salmond.}}]]|series=[[w:Ante-Nicene Fathers|Ante-Nicene Christian Library]]:{{nb...|Translations of the Writings of the Fathers down to A.D. 325}}|seriesvolume=VI (Hippolytus, Bishop of Rome. Vol. I.)|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:T&T Clark|T. & T. Clark]],{{nb...|38, George Street}}; London: Hamilton & Co.; Dublin: John Robertson & Co.|year=1868|section=book IV (Of the Refutation of All Heresies)|page=111|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pX1PAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA111|oclc=490122286|passage=[T]he '''ennead''' is subtracted for this cause, because the three hundred and sixty parts of the entire [circle] consist of '''enneads''', and for this reason the four regions of the world are circumscribed by ninety perfect parts.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} Any [[grouping#Noun|grouping]] or [[system]] containing nine [[object#Noun|objects]].

